#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Per-identity self-service, and the operations plane for the operator.

Two audiences, one process, one authorisation model:

- **Members** review the memories the assistant proposed about *them*, and
  connect or disconnect their own accounts. Sam should never need a terminal
  to say "no, don't remember that" or "use my other Google account".
- **Admins** additionally see connector health and set up the messaging apps
  the gateway talks through.

## Why this exists at all

Private memory proposals used to be approvable by nobody. The review token is
the only route to approval, the operator CLI is the only holder of it, and
having Alex approve Sam's *private* memories would defeat the point of them
being private. The queue was therefore unreachable by design once more than one
person existed. A self-service surface is not a convenience here; it is the
missing half of the review gate.

## Magic links

You type your email address, a link arrives, opening it signs you in. No
password to choose, forget, or reuse — which matters when the people using this
are a household rather than a company.

The link is single-use, expires in ten minutes, is stored only as a hash so
this directory leaking yields nothing usable, is compared in constant time, and
is rate-limited per address.

### Why the link is also bound to your browser

Ordinarily a magic link is a bearer token: whoever holds it is you. That is a
bad fit *here specifically*, because *the assistant can read the inbox the link
is sent to*. It has search_gmail and read_gmail on Alex's account. A
prompt-injected model could search for the message, follow the link, sign in as
Alex, and approve its own memory proposals — and the review gate is its only
route to durable memory, so self-approval collapses it entirely.

Blocking that by hiding the sender from the assistant's Gmail queries would be
an allowlist the assistant could reason its way around. Instead the link is
constrained: requesting one sets a nonce cookie, and redemption requires that
cookie. The link works in the browser that asked for it and nowhere else.

Whoever merely *reads* the link cannot use it. That holds for email, for
Discord, for a screenshot in a chat log — any channel, including ones added
later, rather than a defence aimed at one of them. Discord is just how you talk
to the assistant; it is not part of the trust path.

Nothing here is an MCP tool. The assistant cannot mint a link, list links, or
reach this port.

## Roles

Two, not an engine. `admin` and `member`, deny by default, resolved from
AGENTBOX_ADMINS. A general RBAC system would be a second policy vocabulary
sitting next to approval-policy.yaml, and two policy systems disagree about
who may do what — usually at the moment it matters.

Stdlib only, like everything else on this host.
"""
